# =================================================================
# .env.pbench-scale.example
#
# The 1024-agent SIMULATED scale test on AKS. No LLM backend, no ProgramBench
# cleanroom, no credential you do not generate yourself.
#
#   cp this file to .env.pbench-scale
#   fill every CHANGEME  (openssl rand -hex 32)
#   work through §1-§5 below
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# §1. ACCESS — and the part people get wrong
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Cluster:  agensteam-aks    resource group: h100    region: japaneast
#           node resource group: agensteam-aks-nodes
#
# TWO role assignments are needed, and the first is the one that gets missed:
#
#   a) On the CLUSTER resource:
#        /subscriptions/<sub>/resourceGroups/h100/providers/
#        Microsoft.ContainerService/managedClusters/agensteam-aks
#      Role: "Azure Kubernetes Service Cluster User Role"
#
#      Access to the NODE resource group (agensteam-aks-nodes) is NOT a
#      substitute and will not work. That group holds the VMSS, disks and load
#      balancer; it grants nothing on the API server, so `az aks
#      get-credentials` fails and no kubectl command can run. If you were only
#      given the node group, ask for the role above on the cluster itself.
#
#   b) On the ACR holding the images: "AcrPull", or the registry's admin
#      credential. scripts/pbench_k8s_up.sh builds the pull secret with
#      `az acr credential show`, so an admin-enabled registry needs no role
#      assignment at all (attaching an ACR to a cluster requires Owner, which
#      is why that path is not used here).
#
#   Verify before doing anything else -- both must succeed:
#       az aks get-credentials -g h100 -n agensteam-aks --overwrite-existing
#       kubectl get nodes
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# §2. WHAT THE CLUSTER MUST HAVE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
#   * A POLICY-ENFORCING dataplane. Check:
#       az aks show -g h100 -n agensteam-aks --query networkProfile.networkPolicy
#     Expect "cilium". Without one the NetworkPolicies are ACCEPTED and enforce
#     NOTHING: the agents keep full internet access and the run is silently
#     unsound rather than visibly broken. So probe the behaviour, not the
#     objects, before trusting any result: a pod carrying an agent's labels
#     must fail `curl --max-time 10 https://api.github.com`, and egress-proxy
#     must reach it.
#   * A node pool labelled workload=agents, autoscaler max >= 5 nodes.
#     1024 agents at the CPU request below need ~102 cores.
#   * Storage classes azurefile-csi-premium and managed-csi-premium (AKS default).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# §3. IMAGES — build five, push, then PIN BY DIGEST
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
#     docker build -t omcgitea-mini-router:local     mini-router
#     docker build -t omcgitea-board:local           board-service
#     docker build -t omcgitea-secret-service:local  secret-service
#     docker build -t omcgitea-bootstrap:local       docker/bootstrap
#     docker build -t omcgitea-sim-agent:local -f docker/sim-agent/Dockerfile .
#
# The sim image is FROM the generic agent image, NOT a ProgramBench cleanroom:
# a sim submission scores ~0 by construction, so the ~4 GB per-task images play
# no part in this path.
#
# Tag each as <acr>.azurecr.io/<name>:local, push, then resolve digests:
#     for r in omcgitea-sim-agent omcgitea-mini-router; do
#       d=$(az acr manifest list-metadata -n <acr> -r $r \
#             --query "[?tags[0]=='local'].digest | [0]" -o tsv)
#       printf '%s=%s,' "$r" "$d"
#     done
#     export PBENCH_IMAGE_DIGESTS='omcgitea-sim-agent=sha256:...,omcgitea-mini-router=sha256:...'
#
# Pinning is NOT optional at this size. `:local` is a mutable tag, so without a
# digest the manifests carry imagePullPolicy: Always, 1024 pods each hit the
# registry, and that trips the KUBELET's own registryPullQPS -- ~12% of pods sit
# in ImagePullBackOff ("pull QPS exceeded") behind an exponential backoff that
# never converges.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# §4. THREE EXPORTS (deliberately not stored in this file)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# pbench_k8s_up.sh and tools/gen-k8s.py require these and have NO defaults, so a
# run cannot silently resolve images against somebody else's registry:
#     export PBENCH_ACR=<acr name>
#     export PBENCH_REGISTRY=<acr>.azurecr.io
#     export AZ_SUBSCRIPTION=<subscription id>
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
# §5. RUN IT
# -----------------------------------------------------------------
#     PROFILE=pbench-scale bash scripts/pbench_k8s_up.sh
#       -> namespace pbench-scale, pull secret, ~40 objects, then waits for
#          bootstrap to issue 1024 agent accounts (~10 min)
#     kubectl -n pbench-scale scale statefulset/agent --replicas=1024
#
# EXPECT ~13% of pods to fail their first start with "failed to create shim
# task: context canceled" -- 1024 pods at once outruns containerd. They do not
# self-heal. Delete them in batches; the StatefulSet recreates them onto nodes
# that already hold the image:
#     kubectl -n pbench-scale get pods --no-headers | grep -E 'Error|StartError' \
#       | awk '{print $1}' | head -50 \
#       | xargs -r kubectl -n pbench-scale delete pod --force --grace-period=0
#
# WATCH FOR STALLS, NOT THE READY COUNT. Every failure mode found at this scale
# reported healthy; a ready count that stops climbing is the signal:
#     kubectl -n pbench-scale get pods --no-headers | grep '^agent-' \
#       | awk '{print $3}' | sort | uniq -c
#
# MEASURE from inside the namespace -- a port-forward is one TCP connection
# through the API server and becomes the slowest hop, so you would be timing the
# tunnel rather than the service:
#     kubectl -n pbench-scale create configmap load-observer \
#       --from-file=pbench_load_observe.py=scripts/pbench_load_observe.py
#     # then a pod mounting that configmap plus the pbench-files PVC.
#
# TEAR DOWN:
#     PROFILE=pbench-scale bash scripts/pbench_k8s_down.sh
#     # KEEP=1 stops the workloads and keeps the PVCs
# =================================================================

ORG_NAME=pbench
PROFILE=pbench-scale

# Deployment target. compose = single host (the default everywhere else);
# k8s = this file's path. The two are parallel entry points, not a migration.
PBENCH_ORCHESTRATOR=k8s

# No LLM. The sim drives the real gitea/mattermost/board chain from a scripted
# policy, so the LOAD is real even though the reasoning is not.
AGENT_KIND=sim
AGENT_IMAGE=<your-acr>.azurecr.io/omcgitea-sim-agent:local

# --- topology ------------------------------------------------------------
# Any N works: tools/gen-compose-prod.py emits every worker, its router, the
# bootstrap AGENTS list and the board members from this one value.
PBENCH_WORKERS_PER_TEAM=1024
PBENCH_CI_GATE=0
MERGE_WATCH_REPO=pbench/main
REPOS=main
REQUIRED_APPROVALS=0
MINI_ROUTER_STUCK_MS=1200000

# --- the load under test -------------------------------------------------
# Requests per minute PER AGENT. 1024 agents at GITEA_RPM=1 is ~17 req/s, which
# is what makes gitea's database choice (below) the question worth asking.
PBENCH_SIM_GITEA_RPM=1
PBENCH_SIM_MM_RPM=0.2
PBENCH_SIM_BOARD_RPM=0.5
# A scale test needs steady state, not the sim's default 4-turn burst.
PBENCH_SIM_TURNS=100000

# --- gitea off sqlite ----------------------------------------------------
# sqlite3 is single-writer, and gitea is the write-heavy service here: every
# issue, comment, PR and merge is a write. The pool matters as much as the
# switch -- gitea's default of 2 open connections re-serialises exactly what
# moving to mysql is meant to parallelise.
GITEA_DB_TYPE=mysql
GITEA_DB_MAX_OPEN=200
GITEA_DB_MAX_IDLE=50

# --- ceilings that bind above ~200 agents --------------------------------
# 1024 routers hold ~3 connections each, plus gitea's pool and mattermost's own.
MYSQL_MAX_CONNECTIONS=5000
# Sized to a 1,024-agent fleet's event working set. Smaller profiles retain
# the generator defaults (128 MiB InnoDB buffer pool and 20 GiB MySQL PVC).
MYSQL_INNODB_BUFFER_POOL_SIZE=8589934592
PBENCH_MYSQL_SIZE=512Gi
PBENCH_SC_DISK=managed-csi-premium
# Every agent joins one mattermost team. At the default 200 a 1024-agent stack
# loses everyone past the 200th: bootstrap's add-member call fails per agent,
# those agents get no MM identity, never receive a wake DM, and idle the entire
# run while looking healthy.
MM_MAX_USERS_PER_TEAM=2000

# --- per-agent resources -------------------------------------------------
# The REQUEST is what the scheduler reserves, so it decides packing. 2 cores is
# right for an agent that compiles and wrong by 20x for one that issues a
# request a minute: at 2 cores a D32 holds 15 sim agents, so 1024 of them want
# ~68 nodes -- past the pool ceiling, i.e. the run never schedules at all.
PBENCH_AGENT_CPU=100m
PBENCH_AGENT_MEM=512Mi
PBENCH_AGENT_MEM_LIMIT=2Gi

# --- credentials (CHANGEME: openssl rand -hex 32) ------------------------
GITEA_ADMIN_USER=chairman
GITEA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=CHANGEME
MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=CHANGEME
MYSQL_PASSWORD=CHANGEME
SECRET_SERVICE_INTERNAL_AUTH=CHANGEME
MATTERMOST_ADMIN_USER=mmadmin
MATTERMOST_ADMIN_PASSWORD=CHANGEME

# --- model path: unused here ---------------------------------------------
# AGENT_KIND=sim never calls a model. These stay placeholders, but they are
# still read by the compose render that gen-k8s.py consumes, so they must exist.
ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://egress-proxy:4180
ANTHROPIC_MODEL=sim
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-1234
PBENCH_LLM_UPSTREAM=CHANGEME
UPSTREAM_AUTH_TOKEN=CHANGEME
UPSTREAM_AUTH_STYLE=bearer

# --- anti-cheat ----------------------------------------------------------
# Leave set. On a real task the reference binary is staged root:root 0111 and
# the ACP child runs as this uid, so it can be executed and never read; unsetting
# it lets an agent dump the binary it is meant to reverse-engineer, which voids
# the run. The sim image carries no reference binary, and the initContainer says
# so in its log rather than passing over it silently.
ACP_CHILD_UID=10000
ACP_CHILD_GID=10000

# --- compose-only leftovers ----------------------------------------------
# Ports and subnets mean nothing on k8s -- nothing is published and the CNI owns
# addressing -- but `docker compose config`, which gen-k8s.py renders from,
# requires them to interpolate.
EDGE_HTTP_PORT=19800
EDGE_MM_PORT=19802
EDGE_DOMAIN=localhost
MATTERMOST_SITE_URL=http://localhost:19802
DATA_ROOT=./.claude-team-data
PBENCH_SUBNET_DEFAULT=10.209.0.0/24
PBENCH_SUBNET_EGRESS=10.209.1.0/24
PBENCH_SUBNET_EDGE=10.209.2.0/24
PBENCH_DIRECTOR_AGENT=pbench-worker1
PBENCH_AUDITOR_AGENT=pbench-worker1
PBENCH_TASK=typst__typst.88356d0
